{"content_hash": "851059ec4475d1d9", "version_id": 347, "article_id": 35, "level_code": "ja_n4", "lang": "ja", "level": 2, "level_label": "N4 初級", "title": "家庭の中の暴力：精神の病気がある人による殺人、被害者の多くは家族", "title_zh": "家门里的暴力：精神疾病患者杀人，受害者大多是自家人", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2026/flower-on-a-grave.jpg", "provider": "", "credit": "", "author": "", "credit_url": "", "source": "", "source_url": "", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(93, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(131, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "51edab589bfb"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 422, "reading_minutes": 1.3, "lead": "スペインの大学が、10年間の殺人事件2500件を調べました。その10%は、精神の病気がある人によるものでした。被害者の多くは、家族でした。", "lead_zh": "西班牙一所大学调查了十年间2500起杀人案。其中10%是精神疾病患者所为，而受害者大多是他们的家人。", "preview": "むかしは、精神の病気がある人は「あぶない人」と考えられていました。しかし今は、「その人も被害者だ」と考えるようになりました。この考えは大切ですが、別の問題もあります。家族が殺された人のことが、あまり見えなくなったのです。", "preview_zh": "过去，人们觉得有精神疾病的人是“危险的人”。但现在，人们开始认为“他们也是受害者”。这种想法很重要，不过也带来了另一个问题：被家人杀害的受害者，反而变得不被看见了。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 337, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/35/ja_n4.mp3", "duration": 95.275, "size_bytes": 575424, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 299, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fd8ae74/content/data/versions/347.json", "paragraphs": ["むかしは、精神の病気がある人は「あぶない人」と考えられていました。しかし今は、「その人も被害者だ」と考えるようになりました。この考えは大切ですが、別の問題もあります。家族が殺された人のことが、あまり見えなくなったのです。", "スペインの大学の研究者が、裁判所で審理された殺人事件2500件以上を調べました。10%の事件で、犯人に精神の病気がありました。そのほとんどは家の中で起きました。26%は、親を殺す事件でした。", "研究によると、こうした事件の被害者は、お金の補償をほとんどもらえません。理由の一つは、事件が家族の中で起きることです。家族の人は、体も心も傷つきます。ほかの家族もつらい思いをします。", "研究者のタマリットさんは言います。「家族は、犯人と一番多く会うから、一番あぶない。社会は、この問題を家族にまかせている」。また、被害者は国から十分な助けをもらえないと言います。", "この問題をへらすには、国の医療のしくみが家族をもっと助けることが大切です。あぶないと分かった人を、よく見ていくことも必要です。研究者は、今のやり方を変えるべきだと言っています。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["过去，人们觉得有精神疾病的人是“危险的人”。但现在，人们开始认为“他们也是受害者”。这种想法很重要，不过也带来了另一个问题：被家人杀害的受害者，反而变得不被看见了。", "西班牙一所大学的研究人员调查了法院审理的2500多起杀人案。其中10%的案件里，犯人有精神疾病。这些案件几乎都发生在家里，26%是杀害父母的案件。", "研究显示，这类案件的受害者几乎拿不到金钱赔偿。原因之一是案件发生在家庭内部。家人不仅身体受伤，心里也受伤，其他家庭成员同样痛苦。", "研究人员塔马里特说：“家人和犯人接触最多，所以最危险。社会把这个问题丢给了家庭。”他还说，受害者从国家得到的帮助也不够。", "要减少这类问题，国家的医疗体系需要更多地帮助家庭，也要对已经发现风险的人多加关注。研究人员说，现在的做法应该改变。"], "body": "むかしは、精神の病気がある人は「あぶない人」と考えられていました。しかし今は、「その人も被害者だ」と考えるようになりました。この考えは大切ですが、別の問題もあります。家族が殺された人のことが、あまり見えなくなったのです。\n\nスペインの大学の研究者が、裁判所で審理された殺人事件2500件以上を調べました。10%の事件で、犯人に精神の病気がありました。そのほとんどは家の中で起きました。26%は、親を殺す事件でした。\n\n研究によると、こうした事件の被害者は、お金の補償をほとんどもらえません。理由の一つは、事件が家族の中で起きることです。家族の人は、体も心も傷つきます。ほかの家族もつらい思いをします。\n\n研究者のタマリットさんは言います。「家族は、犯人と一番多く会うから、一番あぶない。社会は、この問題を家族にまかせている」。また、被害者は国から十分な助けをもらえないと言います。\n\nこの問題をへらすには、国の医療のしくみが家族をもっと助けることが大切です。あぶないと分かった人を、よく見ていくことも必要です。研究者は、今のやり方を変えるべきだと言っています。", "body_zh": "过去，人们觉得有精神疾病的人是“危险的人”。但现在，人们开始认为“他们也是受害者”。这种想法很重要，不过也带来了另一个问题：被家人杀害的受害者，反而变得不被看见了。\n\n西班牙一所大学的研究人员调查了法院审理的2500多起杀人案。其中10%的案件里，犯人有精神疾病。这些案件几乎都发生在家里，26%是杀害父母的案件。\n\n研究显示，这类案件的受害者几乎拿不到金钱赔偿。原因之一是案件发生在家庭内部。家人不仅身体受伤，心里也受伤，其他家庭成员同样痛苦。\n\n研究人员塔马里特说：“家人和犯人接触最多，所以最危险。社会把这个问题丢给了家庭。”他还说，受害者从国家得到的帮助也不够。\n\n要减少这类问题，国家的医疗体系需要更多地帮助家庭，也要对已经发现风险的人多加关注。研究人员说，现在的做法应该改变。", "text": "むかしは、精神の病気がある人は「あぶない人」と考えられていました。しかし今は、「その人も被害者だ」と考えるようになりました。この考えは大切ですが、別の問題もあります。家族が殺された人のことが、あまり見えなくなったのです。 スペインの大学の研究者が、裁判所で審理された殺人事件2500件以上を調べました。10%の事件で、犯人に精神の病気がありました。そのほとんどは家の中で起きました。26%は、親を殺す事件でした。 研究によると、こうした事件の被害者は、お金の補償をほとんどもらえません。理由の一つは、事件が家族の中で起きることです。家族の人は、体も心も傷つきます。ほかの家族もつらい思いをします。 研究者のタマリットさんは言います。「家族は、犯人と一番多く会うから、一番あぶない。社会は、この問題を家族にまかせている」。また、被害者は国から十分な助けをもらえないと言います。 この問題をへらすには、国の医療のしくみが家族をもっと助けることが大切です。あぶないと分かった人を、よく見ていくことも必要です。研究者は、今のやり方を変えるべきだと言っています。", "vocab": [{"word": "精神の病気", "pos": "noun", "zh": "精神疾病", "note": "「せいしんのびょうき」"}, {"word": "殺人", "pos": "noun", "zh": "杀人", "note": "「さつじん」"}, {"word": "被害者", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受害者", "note": "「ひがいしゃ」"}, {"word": "家族", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家人、家庭", "note": "「かぞく」"}, {"word": "犯人", "pos": "noun", "zh": "犯人", "note": "「はんにん」"}, {"word": "調べる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "调查", "note": "「しらべる」"}, {"word": "補償", "pos": "noun", "zh": "赔偿", "note": "「ほしょう」"}, {"word": "研究者", "pos": "noun", "zh": "研究人员", "note": "「けんきゅうしゃ」"}, {"word": "助ける", "pos": "verb", "zh": "帮助", "note": "「たすける」"}, {"word": "社会", "pos": "noun", "zh": "社会", "note": "「しゃかい」"}], "title_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders", "summary_original": "In recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.", "body_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders\nby\nOpen University of Catalonia\nedited by\nLisa Lock\n, reviewed by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nIn recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.\nIn this new context, a team of researchers from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) has analyzed homicides committed in Spain to characterize those perpetrated by people with mental disorders and study the factors that determine who the victims are and the compensation awarded in legal proceedings. Their research is\npublished\nin the\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n.\n\"The noble aim of avoiding stigmatizing people with mental disorders as dangerous can contribute to overlooking the serious situation of those who are victims of this violence or face a greater risk of victimization,\" explained Josep Maria Tamarit, a professor of criminal law at the UOC and coordinator of the Empirical and Applied Victimology research group (VICRIM). He coordinated this research together with professors Laura Arantegui and Alba Marsol, who are part of the same group. All three teach in the Faculty of Law and Political Science.\nAnalysis of 2,500 homicides in a decade\nAs part of the study, the researchers from the Digital Transformation and Governance Research Center (UOC-DIGIT) reviewed more than 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. Of these cases, 10% involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. In the vast majority of cases, homicides committed by people with some type of mental disorder occurred in the home, with 26% being parricides (involving the killing of a parent).\nAccording to the experts, the main objective of this open-access study was to shed light on a reality that has so far been overlooked: the lack of recognition and compensation for victims of lethal violence committed by people with mental disorders, an issue obscured by efforts to prevent the stigmatization of the perpetrators. In addition, the study found that only a small number of victims of such crimes received compensation or financial support, a disadvantage largely attributable to the intrafamilial nature of most incidents.\nThe issue is complex: When a person with a mental disorder intentionally assaults a family member, the consequences include both physical and psychological harm. Other family members are also affected.\nFamily ties as the greatest risk factor\nHowever, in many cases, because of this tendency to avoid stigmatizing people with mental disorders, perpetrators are acquitted or receive minimal sentences. Meanwhile, victims, both direct and indirect, are left without any compensation.\n\"Family members are most at risk because they are the people who have the greatest contact with perpetrators. Society leaves this problem in the hands of families. It is also important to consider the tensions that can arise within family relationships, which are intensified by the stress caused by living with people with these types of disorders,\" explained Tamarit, who noted that these victims \"do not receive sufficient support from institutions.\"\nThis legal and contextual situation can be \"considered a systemic failure\" because victims are not compensated. Moreover, in some cases, to obtain effective compensation, they must hire a lawyer and pursue criminal proceedings against a family member with a mental disorder.\n\"We don't have any data to report specific failures, but if one considers that more than 10% of homicides are committed by people who represent no more than 2% of the population, and whose mental health problems are known to the health care system in most cases, we should be able to improve the detection of cases involving risk and the monitoring of individuals and their families,\" said the UOC researcher.\nPreventive and reparative measures\nTo prevent this type of homicide, particularly within the family setting, support for families provided by the public health care system should be strengthened, including follow-up care for cases where risk has been identified.\nIn the experts' view, it is essential to rethink the current approach to victimization and to focus on the specific characteristics of this form of victimization, which frequently occurs within the perpetrator's closest family circle.\n\"The health care system should increase the monitoring of cases, apply risk identification tools and improve the public support system for victims of violent crime. While several reforms have been introduced to improve support in cases of gender-based violence, other needs have so far remained overlooked,\" concluded Tamarit.\nMore information\nJosep Maria Tamarit et al, Invisible harm after lethal violence: Victims of homicide by offenders with mental disorders,\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.jflm.2026.103173\nKey medical concepts\nMental Disorders\nHomicide\nClinical categories\nPsychiatry\nPsychology & Mental health\nProvided by\nOpen University of Catalonia\nWho's behind this story?\nLisa Lock\nBA art history, MA material culture. Former museum editor, paramedic, and transplant coordinator. Editing for Science X since 2021.\nFull profile →\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nHidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T01:22:31.644417", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "むかし", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.55, "sm": 125, "em": 550, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "は", "s": 0.562, "e": 0.787, "sm": 562, "em": 787, "cs": 3, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "、", "s": 0.787, "e": 1.075, "sm": 787, "em": 1075, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "精神", "s": 1.075, "e": 1.587, "sm": 1075, "em": 1587, "cs": 5, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "の", "s": 1.6, "e": 1.713, "sm": 1600, "em": 1712, "cs": 7, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "病気", "s": 1.725